{#
| Variable                  | Type                      | Description |
|---------------------------|---------------------------|-------------|
| page                      | object                    |             |
| page.title                | string                    |             |
| page.slug                 | string                    |             |
| notifications             | Notification[] *Paginator |             |
| notifications.next_page   | ?string                   |             |
| notifications.count       | int                       |             |
| notifications[].is_new    | Bool                      |             |
| notifications[].url       | string                    |             |
| notifications[].sub_title | string                    |             |
| notifications[].title     | string                    |             |
| notifications[].date      | string                    |             |
| user.is_notifiable        | bool                      | the promotional-messages setting, server-rendered |
#}
{#
  T-5.08 — the notifications page.

  `Notifications Page.pdf` draws TWO cards, not one, and T-3.02 already knew it:
  its content column ships `space-y-6` with the comment «the same 24 the
  notifications artboard leaves between its two cards». The first is the
  promotional-messages switch; the second is the list. Both are this task's.

  THE SURFACE IS NOT `.card`, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Sampled
  off the 300dpi render: both cards fill `#F7F6F4` — `--surface-section` — inside
  a 1px `#646361` border, which is `--border-interactive`. The icon tiles inside
  them fill `#FDFDFD`, `--surface-page`. So the notification card is the warm
  panel with a border, **not** T-2.15's white `.card` on `--border-subtle`. Doc
  04 lists «notification» among the six cards sharing the shell; on this artboard
  it does not share it, and inventing a `.card--warm` for one page would be the
  duplicated variant that doc forbids. `.notify-panel` is declared once here and
  used by both cards.

  THE LIST IS `salla-notifications`, RESTYLED FROM OUTSIDE — technique C, and it
  reaches further than usual because **both `salla-notifications` and
  `salla-notification-item` render into the light DOM**. Verified in the loader
  bundle: each is registered with Stencil encapsulation flag `0`, and
  `salla-notifications.css` is a zero-byte file. There is no shadow root between
  the theme's stylesheet and the rows, so no `part` is needed and nothing is
  forked.

  WHAT CSS CANNOT REACH IS IN `partials/notifications.js`, and it is three
  things — the switch's write path, `<time datetime>`, and the unread label.
  See that file; each is there because a stylesheet cannot add an attribute.
#}
{% extends "layouts.customer" %}

{% block head_scripts %}
	{#
	  Its own entry rather than a line in `pages.js`: that bundle is
	  `loyalty.js` + `brands.js`, and this page needs neither.
	#}
	<script defer src="{{ 'notifications.js'|asset }}"></script>
{% endblock %}

{% block inner_content %}
	{% hook 'customer:notifications.items.start' %}

	{#
	  THE PROMOTIONAL-MESSAGES SWITCH, AND WHY IT IS NOT `<salla-user-settings>`.
	  That component carries the same setting — same `is_notifiable`, same
	  `profile/settings` write — but it renders THREE sections: hide-my-name,
	  promotional messages and delete-account, with a subtitle under each. It
	  takes no prop to render one. The artboard draws a single row with no
	  subtitle, so using it would mean shipping two sections this page does not
	  draw and then hiding them, and the delete-account flow belongs on the
	  profile page where upstream already places that component.

	  Nothing is reimplemented by not using it: the label is the platform's own
	  `pages.profile.promotional_messages` key, and `notifications.js` writes
	  through `salla.profile.updateSettings({name, value})` — byte for byte the
	  call Salla's own `toggleSetting()` makes.

	  The initial state is server-rendered from `user.is_notifiable`, the same
	  variable upstream's `profile.twig` reads, so the switch is never briefly
	  wrong while a fetch resolves.
	#}
	<section class="account-panel notify-prefs" aria-labelledby="notify-prefs-title">
		<h2 class="sr-only" id="notify-prefs-title">{{ trans('theme.notifications.preferences') }}</h2>

		<div class="notify-prefs__row">
			<span class="account-tile" aria-hidden="true">
				{% include 'components.ui.icon' with { name: 'special-discount', size: 'lg' } only %}
			</span>

			{% include 'components.ui.choice' with {
				id:      'promotional-messages',
				type:    'switch',
				label:   trans('pages.profile.promotional_messages'),
				checked: user.is_notifiable
			} only %}
		</div>
	</section>

	{#
	  The switch writes to the customer's marketing preference over the network,
	  and a switch that silently fails to save is worse than one that is not
	  there. `notifications.js` announces both outcomes here and reverts the
	  control on failure, which Salla's own component does not do.
	#}
	<span class="sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-notify-status></span>

	<section class="account-panel notifications" aria-labelledby="notify-list-title">
		<h2 class="sr-only" id="notify-list-title">{{ trans('theme.notifications.list') }}</h2>

		<salla-notifications data-testid="store-notifications"></salla-notifications>
	</section>

	{% hook 'customer:notifications.items.end' %}
{% endblock %}
